WEBVTT

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.560
<i>It is the 25th year of the Taisho Era.</i>

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.020
<i>Starting her job at the Imperial Library
Intelligence Asset Management Bureau,</i>

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.990
<i>the eager young woman rushes to
produce results as quickly as possible</i>

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.950
<i>and loses sight of the things around her.</i>

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:19.040
<i>She has comrades here who kindly 
and, at times, strictly look after her.</i>

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.150
<i>This is the story of Kuze 
Tsugumi and her choices.</i>

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:08.200
A blue-eyed doll

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:13.040
made of celluloid was born in America...

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.040
When she arrived at the harbor in Japan...

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.760
Good morning, my princess.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.160
Good morning, Shizuru-san. You're up early.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.670
Actually, I was out all night and just got back.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.440
I see...

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.250
What's this? Something smells delicious.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.170
You're welcome to have some.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.210
Hey, morning.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.620
Good morning.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.680
Morning.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.580
Good morning.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.430
Um, are you two going somewhere?

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.700
Yeah, we're getting breakfast 
at the Sakanoshita restaurant.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.990
It's cheap.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.190
Morning.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:52.430
You too, Akira?

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.740
Sakanoshita.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.690
Well, if you'd like...

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.730
Let's eat!

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.580
Delicious!

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.580
It's better than any restaurant's cooking!

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.300
This miso soup...

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.060
...has so many...

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.090
...ingredients!

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.090
Huh?

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.590
Listen. The miso soup at Sakanoshita
barely has anything in it!

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:24.600
Yes, the best part is the scraps of seaweed.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.100
I've only gotten vegetable scraps in mine.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.650
Sometimes they don't even use soup stock.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.100
Is that even really a restaurant?

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.080
Yes, but it's cheap!

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.320
And fast.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.180
My head is clearer now 
thanks to this miso soup.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.610
I'll be able to write well.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.840
So you spent the night working, then.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.500
Oh, that reminds me.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.120
Yes?

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.190
Were you in the chorus club at your school?

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.120
Yes, I was.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.130
Please allow me to hear your 
singing again, princess.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.100
Huh?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.520
You have a nice voice.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.130
She does?

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.720
She really seemed to enjoy it.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:04.700
She did.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.890
<i>I was singing?</i>

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.890
Shame you missed it, Akira.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.890
Those Who Take the Name of the God of Flames

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.890
-Kagutsuchi-

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.080
Well, that's it for today.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:19.650
Good work.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.140
Kuze, are you free after this?

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:23.150
I am.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.040
What a nice café.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.100
The owner here is a playmate of 
mine from when I studied abroad,

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.300
so I often come here.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.300
Flamant Rose? What lovely name.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.800
May I take your order?

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.810
I'll have the beef curry and a soda.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.810
Hayashi rice and coffee.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.810
I'll have sandwiches and a lemonade.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.320
Then I'll have sandwiches and a milkshake.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.560
Please enjoy.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.320
It's so good.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.030
Do you like milkshakes?

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:11.850
Yes, I love them.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.330
Hey, give that back! Wait!

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.260
Hey! Wait!

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:18.000
Wait!

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.670
Huh?

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.400
We will burn the dangerous books!

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.800
Burn them!

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:33.800
Hey, you there!

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:35.500
Trying to run?

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.160
Hayato!

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.400
I'll head that way!

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.270
Kuze, take care of him!

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:41.360
Okay!

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.360
Are you all right?

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.110
<i>It's not a cursed tome.</i>

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.120
<i>It's actually burning.</i>

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.730
C-Curse them...

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.340
You shouldn't get up just yet.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.330
I'll go summon a doctor now.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.630
No, I'm fine.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.450
I'm actually a doctor in training,

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.630
so I have a sense for these things.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:10.370
I'm Sagisawa Rui from the Imperial 
Capital University School of Medicine.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.640
Pardon me, but are you with the police?

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:18.640
Oh, no... I'm Kuze, from the Imperial Library 
Intelligence Asset Management Bureau.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.660
The Imperial Library?

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.400
Is that the organization everyone's been 
talking about that deals with strange books?

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:25.800
Ah, yes...

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.080
Perfect timing.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.660
I'd like to get this resolved quickly.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:36.950
The men from earlier were talking 
about burning dangerous books,

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.970
but that was a medical text 
I'd finally just gotten.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.460
So, what about the student?

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.520
He won't be reporting it to the police because 
he didn't want to make it a big deal.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.780
The thugs got away, though.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:57.720
Kagutsuchi, huh?

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.540
Oh, he's here.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:05.340
I'm Officer Tsubameno Tarou from the
Safety Division of the Metropolitan Police.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.130
I will be assisting you in your 
activities starting tomorrow.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.680
Thank you for having me.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.000
Well, in short, he's been 
appointed to serve as a liaison

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.740
to the police department, and an assistant.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.910
A liaison and...

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.750
...assistant?

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:24.250
C-Communication and routine 
duties are important work.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:25.840
I know what this is.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.560
To put it simply, you were 
told to watch us, weren't you?

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:29.550
No, that's not—

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.010
I see. Understood.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.600
Well, we are shunned by 
the higher-ups, after all.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.950
No! That's not the case at all!

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.040
There's also the incident with Kagutsuchi...

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.430
What is Kagutsuchi?

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.770
You ran into them earlier.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.680
A group calling themselves "Kagutsuchi"
has been behind several recent incidents,

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.320
stealing books from people 
and burning them on the spot.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.910
Just like they did today.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:57.420
The public perceives cursed tomes

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:01.910
as terrifying cursed books that 
will kill you if you read them.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.990
Burn them because they're dangerous.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.690
From an emotional perspective, 
it <i>is</i> understandable...

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:10.670
So they turn to vigilante justice because 
they've been sucked in by the rumors.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:12.740
I've been assigned to this post as

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.180
an anticipatory measure, to ensure cooperation

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.180
between the Metropolitan Police and Fukurou.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:18.460
Yeah?

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.820
Well, why not? I can see you're almost 
too honest for your own good.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:23.690
Glad to have you.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:25.800
<i>Kagutsuchi...</i>

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:33.280
Good afternoon.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:38.530
Good afternoon, Perry.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.050
All by yourself today, princess?

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.740
Yes, I thought I should 
familiarize myself with things.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:46.580
Oh?

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.660
Then how about we have a love affair?

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.090
I'm ready anytime.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.340
I'll pass, thank you.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.600
A kaleidoscope?

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:00.620
Yes.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.550
You sell things like that, too?

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.370
A medical school student with 
very skilled hands made it.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.240
I keep it here to sell since it's so well-made.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.180
It's so pretty.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.220
Oh, speak of the devil.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:17.900
Good afternoon.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:19.940
You're...

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.380
Sagisawa-san?

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:27.750
Thank you for your help yesterday.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.020
How are your injuries?

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:33.370
Well, unfortunately, I have a small bump.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:34.760
Bump?

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.460
Never mind... Still, I was surprised.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.510
I never dreamed I'd get caught up 
in that stuff from the rumors.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.770
Are people really talking about it that much?

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.080
I believe the suicide attempt by Ukai-kun,

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.770
the Prime Minister's son, 
played a large part in that.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:57.940
After that, rumors quickly spread of strange 
books that will kill you if you read them.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:01.270
Um, cursed tomes aren't like that.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.800
They're called cursed tomes?

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.790
I also want to know about them in more detail.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:09.420
Sagisawa-san?

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:10.750
I can't allow it.

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:14.710
Not the existence of books that 
could take the life of the reader,

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.800
nor the people who write them.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:19.780
Still, I'm glad.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:24.680
I was able to meet you, thanks to all this.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:27.890
While I have you here,

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:30.870
I'd like to know more about
the cursed tomes, you,

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:34.040
and the Imperial Library Intelligence 
Asset Management Bureau.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:37.940
I'm impressed that you could
remember that name.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:41.690
I don't forget the things I'm interested in.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:46.330
You can call it Fukurou. 
That's what everyone else calls it.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.380
Got it. Then that makes you 
Kuze-san from Fukurou.

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.800
How'd it go walking around on your own?

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.360
I think I'll be able to handle it.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.860
I see. That's reassuring.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.570
I hate the light. I hate the light.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.250
I have to get back to the darkness.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:07.360
What's wrong?

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.040
That man has a cursed tome!

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:11.300
Hey, you!

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.360
I hate the light!

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:19.220
After him!

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:20.360
Kuze!

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:22.360
Right!

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.370
<b>Subway Station</b>

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:25.370
I hate the light!

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:30.800
Hey, wait!

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.380
Come back here!

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:34.760
Hey, you!

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:39.880
Get back here, quick!

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.890
I hate the light!

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:09.240
These books...

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.230
I can see a dark, intense aura 
coming from both of them.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:13.630
I knew it.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:15.190
So you see the same thing.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:15.630
Huh?

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.220
So... where is this cursed tome from?

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:21.540
This one was involved in a separate incident.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:26.060
I believe these two books were 
intentionally created as cursed tomes.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.420
As you know,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.640
a cursed tome is a handwritten book that

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.770
contains the strong emotions of
the author at the time of writing.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:38.760
It's considered likely that 
their occurrence is accidental.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.280
But what if there was a way 
to create them intentionally?

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.030
And what if there are people doing that?

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:53.870
Why would someone do that?!

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:55.790
This was dropped at the scene.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:57.290
Karasu, huh?

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:03.820
Feathers like this were found in previous 
cases of cursed tomes with black auras.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:09.850
So we took to calling this very self-assertive, 
unidentified group "Karasu," or crow.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.460
We don't have any confirmation

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.180
that they're intentionally 
making cursed tomes, though.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:24.970
Kuze, are you awake?

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:29.450
Hello, this is Tsugumi.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:32.800
<i>Tsugumi-sama, Hitaki-sama... 
Hitaki-sama has...</i>

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:36.710
Really, Gramps?

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:40.460
It's good to hear that your brother 
regained consciousness.

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.680
Yes, though he's still not permitted visitors.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.470
Still a relief, at any rate.

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:46.780
Thanks.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.220
<b>Takaya Bookstore</b>

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:53.240
Tsugumi-chan?

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:56.730
Koruri-chan!

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.620
It's been so long! Are you doing well?

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.760
Yes! How have you been?

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:06.900
Actually, I'm working as a journalist 
for the Imperial Capital Newspaper.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:09.990
A journalist? That's incredible!

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.130
I'm still in training,

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:17.720
but I think women ought to get much
more involved in society going forward.

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:20.000
Of course, I never thought I'd see the day...

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:26.710
when I'd see you wearing
a skirt above your knees.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:29.940
Wait, is that a Fukurou uniform?!

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.150
You know it?

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:34.170
I may be in training, but I <i>am</i> more 
or less a newspaper reporter.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.140
Information comes with the territory.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.140
Oh, darn.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:40.640
We'll have to catch up again 
some other time, Tsugumi-chan.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.940
Here. Okay, I'll see you soon!

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:44.650
Okay, see you.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:50.660
<b>Imperial Capital Newspaper
Local News Division

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:52.140
Kuze-san.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:54.300
Sagisawa-san?

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.910
Was that a friend of yours?

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:59.250
Yes, since we were children.
She was my senior at the girls' school.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.310
I see.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.360
Are you going somewhere?

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.670
I was waiting for you.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.010
Huh?

00:15:09.850 --> 00:15:11.460
What is this about?

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:12.830
Here.

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:13.920
This is...

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.450
I really wanted to give it to you.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:18.170
Thank you.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.440
It really is beautiful.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:26.720
Notice how the things you see change 
each time you turn it or look into it?

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:31.980
I think that's probably why I'm so
fascinated by kaleidoscopes.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.200
Sagisawa-san...

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.720
You can call me Rui.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:39.960
I'd like to get more familiar with you.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:47.200
C-Come to think of it... R-Rui... you're 
a student at the medical school, right?

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:48.000
Yeah.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:51.480
Is it because your father 
is a doctor, or something?

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.300
My father was a small-town doctor,

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:56.220
and my mother was a midwife.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:58.870
It's because I grew up watching my parents

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:02.370
save so many lives and 
helping others be born.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:06.030
Are you parents still practicing now?

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.600
They both passed away in an accident.

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:15.550
When they died, all I could think was 
that it happened much too soon.

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:18.570
Um... I'm sorry, I...

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:22.000
It's fine. It happened three years ago.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:23.000
Don't worry about it.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:25.610
Okay.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.870
Ever since, I've been investigating 
the cursed tomes as much as possible.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:37.240
The author's emotions and memories 
held within the books

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.000
influence the people who read them.

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:42.930
At first, I couldn't believe it,

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.820
but I guess such dangerous books 
really are circulating.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:50.250
That's why those of us at Fukurou 
are collecting and saving them.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.540
We remove the dangerous parts and 
copy the contents of the books.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:58.700
But if you can't tell which
books are dangerous,

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:02.660
then you could understand burning 
books with that potential, right?

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:04.930
I know it's not my place to say, but...

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:10.150
I don't think it's right to burn a book just 
because it has the <i>potential</i> to be dangerous.

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:13.420
I love books.

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:15.600
The authors' feelings are written in them,

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:18.680
and they give so many different 
things to those who read them.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.600
That won't change in the future, either.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:24.000
But if you can't tell which ones are...

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:24.980
I can.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:25.690
Huh?

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:29.790
I have the ability to recognize cursed tomes.

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:33.190
I joined Fukurou to make use of that ability.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:39.950
We weren't able to find any 
cursed tomes today, either.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:42.040
Nor do we have any information.

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:46.080
We have a problem, everyone!

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:47.650
Why do you look so alarmed?

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.200
We have reports from citizens that

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.630
a group believed to be Kagutsuchi entered 
a building in the 8th block of Ginza!

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:27.500
A black cursed tome!

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.600
We're with the Imperial Library and Fukurou!

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:30.730
Don't move!

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.260
Kuze, go contact Akira and the 
others downstairs! I'll handle this!

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:40.010
Right!

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:44.440
After them!

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:45.390
Right!

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:47.640
Damn you!

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.460
Stay still!

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:55.900
Naming yourselves Kagutsuchi, 
after the god of flames?

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:58.780
How dare you proclaim such 
arrogant self-righteousness?!

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.670
Just give it up already.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.080
That's a dangerous object.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:39.940
Please give it to me, and Fukurou 
will keep it safely in storage.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:42.810
You really can tell, can't you?

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.440
What's the meaning of this?

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.710
This is a dangerous object, right?

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:50.830
So shouldn't we get rid of it?

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:53.560
Maybe it is tempting to think that way,

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:56.840
but the book's contents aren't to blame.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:59.820
They should be kept after the 
source of evil has been removed.

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.940
So please let us at Fukurou handle it.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:07.720
I understand your ideals, 
but what about the reality?

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:11.100
The contents of the books aren't the problem.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.600
There are those who are exploiting this.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:16.780
You're familiar with them 
too, right? Like Karasu.

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:22.360
That why we, Kagutsuchi, will eliminate them.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:25.870
The author's feelings have no bearing on this.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.900
Is that you, Rui?

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:32.210
Come with us.

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:36.200
We... no, I need you.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.260
I... I won't go with you.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.890
I just don't believe that 
what you're doing is right!

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:51.840
Kuze!

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:52.710
Hayato!

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.640
What a shame.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:55.580
Wait!

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:57.660
But we will meet again...

00:20:57.660 --> 00:20:58.150
Wait!

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.030
Tsugumi.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:11.790
Rui?

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.700
Kuze, where's the cursed tome?

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:25.570
I see...

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.460
<i>Hello, this is Nabari Yutaka.</i>

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:36.330
Chapter 4:  "Nightingale Ball -Nachtigal-"

00:23:22.460 --> 00:23:24.680
<i>Since you scolded me the other day,</i>

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:27.430
<i>I've been thinking about eating properly.</i>

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.380
<i>It's just such a hassle to eat when 
I'm immersed in my research.</i>

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:32.080
<i>What shall I do?</i>

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:36.160
<i>I heard you're paying a visit to the Nightingale in Borrowed Feathers.</i>

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:37.680
<i>Be sure to take care.</i>

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:38.980
<i>See you soon, then.</i>
